@meta
id: 10-04
title: Drill — ciw until it is reflex
chapter: 10
type: drill
skills: obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 18
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: double-click, type, repeat
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
Eight times: the variable is called `record` and it should be called
`order`. Land on the word, change it, save, quit.

Do not aim at the first letter. Land in the middle of the word on
purpose — the point of the drill is that it does not matter.

@keys
| `w` | forward a word |
| `ciw` | change the word you are inside |
| `Esc` `:wq` | out, and save |

@hints
`w` puts you on `record` in one press from the start of the line.
--
`ciw` then type the new name. Do not delete anything by hand first.

@solution
    w ciw order <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes, five of them the new name.

`ciw` is the single most-used text object in vim, and this is why: renaming
one occurrence of an identifier is something you do dozens of times a day,
and it costs three keys plus the word. It replaces the double-click, and
unlike the double-click it works from anywhere inside the word, never
selects the `.` or the quotes by accident, and leaves your hands on the
home row.

Chapter 11 adds `.` to repeat the last change, which turns this drill into
the fastest rename in the editor. Get the three keys automatic first.

@start order.repository.ts
const record = await this.model.findOne({ where: { id } });

@expected order.repository.ts
const order = await this.model.findOne({ where: { id } });
